// An untyped `[]` whose only other write is a `slice` of itself. The
// frontend admits it, and the go reference REFUSES it by name: a `.slice` on a
// receiver of unknown type fixes no element type, so nothing types the literal
// and Go would reject the module (issue #2898).
//
// The write is neither another `[]`, nor a bare local, nor a self-concat whose
// argument is a local, so the only-sized check fails on the write itself even
// before the read of the receiver is considered; and the assignment's inferred
// value type is empty (a builtin keyed on an unknown receiver), so it offers
// no declared target either. This is the refusal a workaround around the
// inference would otherwise let through as `[]any{}`.
//
// Not a byte-agreement case, so it cannot live in the tier's CORPUS.
fn sliced() -> Int {
  var out = []
  out = out.slice(0, 1)
  return out.length()
}
